# Cursor Rules — Interview Lab

## Project context
This is an interview-first local development environment.
Code quality must be "explainable in 30 seconds to a senior engineer".

## Code style
- TypeScript everywhere (strict mode)
- No `any` types — use `unknown` + type guards if needed
- Prefer functional components and hooks over class components
- Keep components under 150 lines; extract if longer
- Use named exports (not default exports) for components and hooks
- Co-locate styles with components (Tailwind classes inline or cva)

## Interview-specific rules
- Every utility function must have a JSDoc comment explaining WHY it exists
- Every custom hook must have a one-line usage comment at the top
- Prefer simple, readable code over clever optimizations
- Avoid premature abstraction — write it twice before extracting
- No external API calls in demos — use mock data / fixtures

## File naming
- Components: PascalCase.tsx
- Hooks: camelCase.ts (must start with `use`)
- Utils: camelCase.ts
- Types: camelCase.types.ts or shared-types package
- Tests: [name].test.ts or [name].spec.ts

## Import order (auto-sorted by Prettier)
1. Node built-ins
2. External packages
3. Workspace packages (@interview-lab/*)
4. Relative imports

## AI assistance rules
- When generating components, always include TypeScript props interface
- When generating hooks, always include return type annotation
- When generating API routes, always include error handling
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- When in doubt about complexity, ask: "can I explain this in an interview?"

## Performance guidelines
- Use React.memo() only when profiling proves it's needed
- Avoid useEffect for derived state — compute inline
- Prefer CSS transitions over JS animations for simple cases
- Use Framer Motion for complex animations (Phase 2+)

## Testing guidelines
- Unit test: pure functions and hooks (vitest)
- Component test: user interactions only (React Testing Library)
- E2E test: critical user flows only (Playwright)
- No snapshot tests
